WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.750
&gt;&gt; Hello, I'm Chris Granade from

00:00:24.751 --> 00:00:26.180
the Quantum Architectures and

00:00:26.181 --> 00:00:28.305
Computation Group
here at Microsoft.

00:00:28.306 --> 00:00:31.400
Today, I'll be talking about
the Quantum Development Kit,

00:00:31.401 --> 00:00:32.975
our recent effort to

00:00:32.976 --> 00:00:35.450
empower people to get
hands-on and learning

00:00:35.451 --> 00:00:40.135
with quantum programming
at a high level.

00:00:40.136 --> 00:00:41.660
There's a number of
different pieces of

00:00:41.661 --> 00:00:44.265
the Quantum Development
Kit including

00:00:44.266 --> 00:00:46.970
comprehensive documentation
to help you get started,

00:00:46.971 --> 00:00:49.830
but the most exciting
piece is Q-sharp,

00:00:49.831 --> 00:00:51.710
our new language for thinking

00:00:51.711 --> 00:00:54.350
about quantum algorithms
at a high level.

00:00:54.351 --> 00:00:57.380
So, let's go on and get started.

00:00:57.381 --> 00:01:05.675
Q-sharp should at some level
be a familiar language.

00:01:05.676 --> 00:01:08.660
We can break things down
into operations, functions,

00:01:08.661 --> 00:01:10.370
namespaces, other elements that

00:01:10.371 --> 00:01:12.560
are familiar from
other languages.

00:01:12.561 --> 00:01:15.770
So, for instance, if we want
to run ''Hello, World!'',

00:01:15.771 --> 00:01:17.120
we can do that by using

00:01:17.121 --> 00:01:20.510
the message function to
print out to a console.

00:01:20.511 --> 00:01:25.550
Once we have that, we can
use that from C-sharp and

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:29.430
Visual Studio in a manner
very similar to how

00:01:29.431 --> 00:01:30.470
you might send code to

00:01:30.471 --> 00:01:33.815
a GPU or an FPGA
as an accelerator.

00:01:33.816 --> 00:01:36.405
Here, we'll ask for
a target machine,

00:01:36.406 --> 00:01:38.070
a quantum simulator,

00:01:38.071 --> 00:01:39.950
that is something
running locally on

00:01:39.951 --> 00:01:41.870
our desktop that simulates how

00:01:41.871 --> 00:01:43.910
an ideal quantum computer
would work for

00:01:43.911 --> 00:01:47.490
small numbers of
qubits up to about 30.

00:01:47.491 --> 00:01:51.200
We can ask our target machine,
our quantum simulator,

00:01:51.201 --> 00:01:53.030
to go and run our new operation

00:01:53.031 --> 00:01:54.420
and wait for the answer back.

00:01:54.421 --> 00:01:57.805
So, if we do that, we'll
see a ''Hello, World!''

00:01:57.806 --> 00:01:59.160
just as we expect.

00:01:59.161 --> 00:02:02.295
So, it's a good hands-on way
to get started with.

00:02:02.296 --> 00:02:03.560
Of course, a quantum

00:02:03.561 --> 00:02:05.180
programming language
wouldn't be very

00:02:05.181 --> 00:02:08.090
interesting or useful if you
couldn't allocate qubits.

00:02:08.091 --> 00:02:11.155
So, let's do that. We can
allocate a new qubit.

00:02:11.156 --> 00:02:13.519
We can use what's
called a Hadamard

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.200
operation to prepare

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.840
a superposition state
on that new qubit.

00:02:17.841 --> 00:02:19.885
Then if we were to measure it,

00:02:19.886 --> 00:02:22.260
we'll get 50-50 statistics

00:02:22.261 --> 00:02:24.210
out between
a zero or one outcome,

00:02:24.211 --> 00:02:26.595
so that makes a good
random number generator.

00:02:26.596 --> 00:02:29.015
So, let's simulate
how that would work.

00:02:29.016 --> 00:02:31.455
We'll allocate a qubit,
apply a Hadamard,

00:02:31.456 --> 00:02:33.560
measure the result, and then
that's the result we can

00:02:33.561 --> 00:02:35.870
return back out to
the classical rolled.

00:02:35.871 --> 00:02:39.005
Once we have that, it's

00:02:39.006 --> 00:02:41.350
very similar at
the C-sharp level.

00:02:41.351 --> 00:02:43.400
We can save out
the result from having

00:02:43.401 --> 00:02:45.875
run our new operation
into a variable.

00:02:45.876 --> 00:02:48.110
We can loop over, we can
print it to a console,

00:02:48.111 --> 00:02:50.140
we can do whatever we'd
like with it at that point,

00:02:50.141 --> 00:02:51.590
giving us a very flexible way of

00:02:51.591 --> 00:02:53.950
interacting with
this new funding routine.

00:02:53.951 --> 00:02:56.710
So, let's run it and
see what we get.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:02.840
So, we get about
the 50-50 statistics

00:03:02.841 --> 00:03:04.435
we'd expect out of that,

00:03:04.436 --> 00:03:08.135
showing that operates
the way we'd expect.

00:03:08.136 --> 00:03:11.330
We can build on that
and do even more

00:03:11.331 --> 00:03:14.080
by using the other operations
built into Q-sharp

00:03:14.081 --> 00:03:16.640
such as the controlled-NOT
operation to prepare

00:03:16.641 --> 00:03:18.800
entangled states instead of

00:03:18.801 --> 00:03:20.940
simply superposition states
on the single qubit.

00:03:20.941 --> 00:03:23.245
So, now, we'll
allocate two qubits,

00:03:23.246 --> 00:03:27.560
apply Hadamard to the first
to prepare a superposition,

00:03:27.561 --> 00:03:29.780
and then make an entangled pair

00:03:29.781 --> 00:03:32.320
using the controlled-NOT
operation, I mentioned earlier.

00:03:32.321 --> 00:03:33.495
Now, when we measure,

00:03:33.496 --> 00:03:35.075
we know that because
they're entangled,

00:03:35.076 --> 00:03:37.420
we'll get the same answer
on both qubits.

00:03:37.421 --> 00:03:40.410
So, we'll return that out
to the classical rolled,

00:03:40.411 --> 00:03:44.400
pull it from C-sharp
in the same fashion.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.065
Again, we get the same kinds
of 50-50 stats we expect.

00:03:51.066 --> 00:03:52.640
Except now correlated between

00:03:52.641 --> 00:03:55.160
the two things with
that entanglement.

00:03:55.161 --> 00:03:56.660
We can also run in

00:03:56.661 --> 00:04:00.230
Visual Studio Code
on not only Windows,

00:04:00.231 --> 00:04:02.250
but also MacOS and Linux.

00:04:02.251 --> 00:04:04.925
So, let's look at

00:04:04.926 --> 00:04:07.660
using the elements that we
just saw in Visual Studio,

00:04:07.661 --> 00:04:10.610
here to build up
something that's actually

00:04:10.611 --> 00:04:13.830
really useful quantum
building block

00:04:13.831 --> 00:04:16.100
as we look at other algorithms.

00:04:16.101 --> 00:04:18.545
So, in particular, let's
start with teleportation.

00:04:18.546 --> 00:04:20.420
That's a little bit
of a whimsical name,

00:04:20.421 --> 00:04:23.450
but it really points to
a very basic building block

00:04:23.451 --> 00:04:24.830
that gets used in a lot of

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:27.050
different contexts within
quantum programming,

00:04:27.051 --> 00:04:31.075
as a way to move information
around the quantum device.

00:04:31.076 --> 00:04:33.930
So, we can write out
the same things we did earlier,

00:04:33.931 --> 00:04:35.050
if a Hadamard and a

00:04:35.051 --> 00:04:37.020
controlled-NOT to prepare
some entanglement.

00:04:37.021 --> 00:04:39.920
We'll do the opposite to
unprepare some entanglement to

00:04:39.921 --> 00:04:42.930
make a measurement that lets
us move that information.

00:04:42.931 --> 00:04:45.200
We'll then apply a correction
based on that measurement,

00:04:45.201 --> 00:04:47.720
and we can use standard
classical techniques

00:04:47.721 --> 00:04:50.270
such as unit testing
to build up confidence

00:04:50.271 --> 00:04:52.940
that we actually did
what we intended to do

00:04:52.941 --> 00:04:55.860
here and actually moved
information correctly.

00:04:55.861 --> 00:05:02.930
So, let's run that.

00:05:02.931 --> 00:05:05.420
Here, we can run it
using the.Net Core SDK,

00:05:05.421 --> 00:05:08.110
the same way we'd
run anything else.

00:05:08.111 --> 00:05:09.400
So, it's C-sharp or F-sharp

00:05:09.401 --> 00:05:11.470
on your favorite
operating system.

00:05:11.471 --> 00:05:13.415
When we run that, we get that

00:05:13.416 --> 00:05:16.360
the state was teleported
successfully.

00:05:16.361 --> 00:05:18.365
But, this somehow it
feels a little bit

00:05:18.366 --> 00:05:20.900
unsatisfying as an ending point
because I had to list

00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:23.060
out each of the individual
operations and that's

00:05:23.061 --> 00:05:25.690
great if you understand
deeply what each of those do.

00:05:25.691 --> 00:05:29.320
But, it really isn't speaking
to how do we get hands-on,

00:05:29.321 --> 00:05:33.470
thinking at a high level
about quantum algorithms.

00:05:33.471 --> 00:05:36.770
So instead, Q-sharp lets
us do something else,

00:05:36.771 --> 00:05:38.480
which is extract those into

00:05:38.481 --> 00:05:40.810
a new operation, document them,

00:05:40.811 --> 00:05:43.400
and give a name

00:05:43.401 --> 00:05:45.700
to those patterns that occur
in front of programming.

00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:48.540
The same way that we might
refactor code classically.

00:05:48.541 --> 00:05:51.335
Now, instead of listing
Hadamard C-NOT,

00:05:51.336 --> 00:05:53.415
I say prepare some entanglement.

00:05:53.416 --> 00:05:55.880
Now, the description
of teleportation say,

00:05:55.881 --> 00:05:58.340
it reads more logically
in terms of what we're

00:05:58.341 --> 00:06:00.380
trying to implement rather

00:06:00.381 --> 00:06:01.640
than how we're
trying to implement.

00:06:01.641 --> 00:06:04.260
So, now, I can say prepare
an entangled pair,

00:06:04.261 --> 00:06:06.700
add joint says to unprepare,

00:06:06.701 --> 00:06:09.315
so that we can use that
preparation as a measurement.

00:06:09.316 --> 00:06:10.950
We can then apply the correction,

00:06:10.951 --> 00:06:14.255
and call this operation
to do teleportation

00:06:14.256 --> 00:06:16.550
as a library routine

00:06:16.551 --> 00:06:19.930
went for a particular state
that we want to send.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:27.250
I can run that in the same way,

00:06:27.251 --> 00:06:31.580
and I'll see that it succeeds
for the exact same reason.

00:06:31.581 --> 00:06:34.400
Once we have these kinds
of building blocks,

00:06:34.401 --> 00:06:36.585
we can use them to do
some really neat things.

00:06:36.586 --> 00:06:39.030
So for instance, if you're
interested in chemistry,

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:42.020
a problem you might
want to ask is given

00:06:42.021 --> 00:06:43.520
some Hamiltonian to describe

00:06:43.521 --> 00:06:45.205
a particular chemical system.

00:06:45.206 --> 00:06:47.645
What is the ground state
of that Hamiltonian?

00:06:47.646 --> 00:06:49.390
What is the ground state energy?

00:06:49.391 --> 00:06:51.560
This is something that
determines a lot of

00:06:51.561 --> 00:06:54.150
different properties
such as reaction rates.

00:06:54.151 --> 00:06:56.410
So, it's really interesting in

00:06:56.411 --> 00:06:58.575
a lot of different
chemistry applications.

00:06:58.576 --> 00:07:03.010
So, let's start with
a simple H2 molecule,

00:07:03.011 --> 00:07:05.790
that where we can compare
the theory and we'll

00:07:05.791 --> 00:07:07.795
describe the initial state

00:07:07.796 --> 00:07:10.605
of a simulation of that molecule,

00:07:10.606 --> 00:07:13.730
sorry, each step
of the simulation,

00:07:13.731 --> 00:07:15.610
the initial state
of that simulation,

00:07:15.611 --> 00:07:17.990
and the statistical techniques,

00:07:17.991 --> 00:07:21.800
we'll use to extract information
about the ground state.

00:07:21.801 --> 00:07:23.900
I can run this in

00:07:23.901 --> 00:07:25.530
exactly the same sense as I

00:07:25.531 --> 00:07:28.080
run all of the basic
building blocks,

00:07:28.081 --> 00:07:31.410
we saw earlier using
the.NET Core SDK.

00:07:31.411 --> 00:07:33.330
We'll see a plot that

00:07:33.331 --> 00:07:40.790
compares the ground state energy

00:07:40.791 --> 00:07:43.155
to be from a quantum
simulation to

00:07:43.156 --> 00:07:45.570
what we know from theory
for this simple molecule,

00:07:45.571 --> 00:07:47.180
giving us some
confidence again that

00:07:47.181 --> 00:07:49.620
we've built up the simulation
in a useful way.

00:07:49.621 --> 00:07:51.530
So, these are some
of the things you

00:07:51.531 --> 00:07:53.920
can do with the Quantum
Development Kit.

00:07:53.921 --> 00:07:57.650
It's all powered
by a large set of

00:07:57.651 --> 00:07:59.660
open source libraries that

00:07:59.661 --> 00:08:01.775
formed the standard library
that you can download,

00:08:01.776 --> 00:08:03.000
look at, see how they work,

00:08:03.001 --> 00:08:04.340
peel back the layers,

00:08:04.341 --> 00:08:05.900
and also contribute to it.

00:08:05.901 --> 00:08:09.005
We're very thankful to
the Q-sharp community for

00:08:09.006 --> 00:08:10.790
many of the contributions
we've gotten

00:08:10.791 --> 00:08:13.415
back into the standard library.

00:08:13.416 --> 00:08:17.325
You can always look and
see how, for instance,

00:08:17.326 --> 00:08:20.000
state preparation for
arbitrary states is defined

00:08:20.001 --> 00:08:22.210
by looking at
the Q-sharp source code

00:08:22.211 --> 00:08:23.550
in the standard libraries.

00:08:23.551 --> 00:08:27.510
To try and help make it easier
to get hands-on and learn,

00:08:27.511 --> 00:08:29.580
whether you're new
to quantum entirely,

00:08:29.581 --> 00:08:31.840
or whether you're a quantum
researcher looking to branch

00:08:31.841 --> 00:08:34.775
into more
programming-based efforts,

00:08:34.776 --> 00:08:36.845
we've recently also released

00:08:36.846 --> 00:08:39.320
a set of what we call Katas or

00:08:39.321 --> 00:08:42.350
self-paced exercises to teach

00:08:42.351 --> 00:08:45.170
different concepts in
quantum programming.

00:08:45.171 --> 00:08:48.590
Starting from what are the
basic operations available to

00:08:48.591 --> 00:08:50.330
a quantum programming and ranging

00:08:50.331 --> 00:08:52.550
through more advanced
topics such as,

00:08:52.551 --> 00:08:55.100
how do you represent
classical functions as

00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:58.630
oracles or subroutines
in a quantum program.

00:08:58.631 --> 00:09:03.525
So, we really hope
you'll give it a try,

00:09:03.526 --> 00:09:05.090
and we look forward

00:09:05.091 --> 00:09:08.240
to your feedback.
Thank you very much.

